{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794154037,"nonce":"KgmpKeFjRaU2IrW0ctuNbXyAtHY=","message":"2026-11-08, why the iPhone fps dropped during long test sessions.\n\nA Metal trace of the running app (8 s, at rest) reports device thermal state Serious for the whole trace: iOS caps CPU and GPU clocks hard at that level. Per-pass GPU time on that throttled phone: \"Blinc Render Pass\" 20 ms median (p90 29), \"Blinc Text Render Pass\" 0.11 ms. The earlier 68 s scrolling capture shows the same slide (60 fps for 30 s, then 44 to 56 as the surface wait grows to about 10 ms while CPU time stays at 1 ms). So the late-session drops, and the 26 fps captures after many back-to-back launches, are throttling, not a code regression. Judge performance only from a phone in Nominal thermal state, and read the state from the trace (`device-thermal-state-intervals`).\n\nThe example also redraws the whole screen at 60 fps for as long as it is open (three looping timelines), which is itself a steady GPU load; a real app at rest would not.\n\nLanded since the shader specialisation: the main pass clears to a full-target opaque background rect instead of drawing it (about one of the scene's two screens of core fragments), and mobile/example drops the field and filled-button shadows as well as the card shadow. To do once the phone has cooled: re-measure the pass in a Nominal state, then shadow shader switches or a retained scroll layer if it is still more than a few ms.","files":null}]}